the queens, which would restore your game to a very good state of defence: but if, instead of
pushing this pawn, he had played his knight to his king's rook's fourth square, you should have
taken his king's knight's pawn with your queen, permitting him afterwards to give double check:
but you ought then to take his knight with your pawn, and sacrifice your rook: your situation
would thus be greatly improved.} 9. Nd3 {The king's knight, at his queen's third square. (d)
